+### Merging duplicates
+
+When a customer opens the same problem twice, staff merge the duplicate into the ticket that
+carries on the work. `POST /tickets/:id/merge` (staff) with `{"merge":{"target_id":57}}` closes
+ticket `:id` as a duplicate of ticket 57. It returns `source` and `target`, both as tickets.
+
+- **Who and what.** Both tickets must belong to the same customer, and the target must be open. A
+  ticket cannot be merged into itself. A target that is missing, not a whole number, from another
+  customer or from another business is `422` `validation_failed`, with the reason under
+  `details.target_id`. Another business's source ticket looks missing (`404`). Customers cannot merge.
+- **The duplicate** gets status `closed` and `merged_into_id: 57`. It stays readable, and its comments
+  stay where they were. Its assignee and group are kept, so the cleanup below still applies.
+- **The surviving ticket** keeps its status, assignee, group, priority and email reply address. A
+  merge only adds a note to it.
+- **Closed tickets reject edits.** A staff `PATCH` or a customer reply on a closed ticket is `422`
+  `ticket_closed`, whatever it changes. An email reply to a closed ticket's reply address is ignored.
+  It adds nothing, opens no new request, and leaves its Message-ID free. Merging a closed ticket is
+  refused the same way (`ticket_closed`). A closed ticket cannot be the target either (`target_closed`).
+- **Notes.** Each side gets a note explaining the merge. `source_comment` is for the duplicate and
+  `target_comment` for the surviving ticket. Each takes `body` and `public`. Notes are private unless
+  `"public":true`. A missing body gets default wording, and `"body":""` is a `422`. A public note
+  is visible to the customer and counts as activity. A private one changes nothing the customer sees.
+  Merge notes never send email, even a public note on a request opened by email.
+- **Assignment cleanup still applies to closed tickets.** Suspending a staff member, removing them
+  from a group, or deleting the group unassigns the tickets they had, closed or not.
+- **Merging again.** If the surviving ticket is later merged, the earlier duplicates keep their
+  `merged_into_id`. The links are never rewritten.
+- **Finding closed tickets.** `status=closed` filters the staff list (`GET /tickets`) and the
+  customer's list (`GET /requests`).
